      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the system of linear equations\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3x - 5y = -11,\\\\\n7x + 2y = -12,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwe can use the method of elimination.\n\nFirst, we need to eliminate one of the variables. Let's eliminate \\(y\\). To do this, we will make the coefficients of \\(y\\) in both equations equal in magnitude but opposite in sign. We can achieve this by multiplying the first equation by 2 and the second equation by 5:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2(3x - 5y) = 2(-11),\\\\\n5(7x + 2y) = 5(-12).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n6x - 10y = -22,\\\\\n35x + 10y = -60.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nNext, we add these two equations together to eliminate \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n(6x - 10y) + (35x + 10y) = -22 + (-60).\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side:\n\n\\[\n6x + 35x = 35x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -10y + 10y = 0,\n\\]\n\nwe get:\n\n\\[\n41x = -82.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-82}{41}.\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(x\\), we substitute it back into one of the original equations to solve for \\(y\\). Let's use the first equation \\(3x - 5y = -11\\):\n\n\\[\n3\\left(\\frac{-82}{41}\\right) - 5y = -11.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3 \\cdot -82}{41} - 5y = \\frac{-246}{41} - 5y = -11.\n\\]\n\nRearranging to solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-246}{41} - 5y = -11,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-246}{41} + 11 = 5y,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-246 + 451}{41} = 5y,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{205}{41} = 5y,\n\\]\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{205}{41 \\cdot 5},\n\\]\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{205}{205} = 1.\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(4, -1)}.\n\\]",
